@meta
id: 14-11
title: Count without changing anything
chapter: 14
type: lesson
skills: sub-confirm
par: 92
minutes: 7
vscode: Ctrl-F and read the "3 of 24" badge, three times
open: refund-audit.md

@brief
The audit summary at the top of this file has three blanks: how many
refunds in the table were approved, how many rejected, how many are
still pending. The batch tool refuses a summary that disagrees with
the table, so the numbers have to be right.

Twenty-two rows is too many to count by eye and get right twice. A
substitute has a flag that reports how many matches it would make and
then changes nothing. Use it three times, then fill in the blanks.

Be careful what you count. The prose above the table uses two of the
three words, and a match in a sentence is not a row.

@keys
| `:%s/pattern//n` `Enter` | report the number of matches, change nothing |
| `:%s/pattern//gn` `Enter` | the same, counting every match on a line rather than lines |
| `/TBC` `Enter` then `ciw` | find a blank and replace the word |
| `n` | the next blank |

@hints
The `n` flag turns any substitute into a dry run. The replacement can
be empty because it is never used.
--
Count something that only appears in table rows. The decision column
is always preceded by a bar and a space: `| approved` cannot match the
sentence that says "approved".
--
`:%s/| approved//n` reports 11. The other two the same way. Then
`/TBC`, `ciw`, the number, and `n` to the next blank.

@solution
    :%s/| approved//n<Enter>   :%s/| rejected//n<Enter>   :%s/| pending//n<Enter>
    /TBC<Enter>  ciw11<Esc>  n ciw7<Esc>  n ciw4<Esc>  :wq<Enter>

Eighty keystrokes, and none of them were spent scrolling up and
down the table with a finger on the screen.

The `n` flag is the dry run. Vim finds every match, reports "7 matches
on 7 lines", and leaves the buffer exactly as it was. Two uses. The
obvious one is this: you need a number. The important one is checking
a pattern before you trust it with a change. If `:%s/\<status\>//gn`
says 11 and you expected 7, you have learned something before it cost
anything, and you can go and look for the four you did not know about.

The pattern here deserves a second look. `\<rejected\>` would have
counted 8, because the second sentence says "Rejected refunds". The
bar and the space in front of the word are what make it a table cell
and not a word. Counting is only as honest as the pattern, and a
pattern that describes the structure around the word is usually the
one that is honest.

